{
  // Static-asset Worker for the built Jekyll site, deployed by Cloudflare
  // Workers Builds from the "Deploy non-production branches" trigger. Its
  // job is per-branch previews: the trigger's deploy command is
  // `npx wrangler versions upload`, which uploads a VERSION without routing
  // traffic to it. Production traffic is unaffected by anything here.
  //
  // The service name MUST NOT be `algosize`. That is the API Worker's
  // service (see worker/wrangler.toml), which GitHub Actions deploys from
  // `.github/workflows/worker.yml`. With both configs named `algosize`, the
  // two pipelines deploy different Workers to the same service and the last
  // one to run wins — so an ordinary site change could silently replace the
  // live API, and an API deploy could replace the site.
  //
  // Related: wrangler resolves its config by walking UP the directory tree,
  // so this file also shadows worker/wrangler.toml for any wrangler command
  // run from inside `worker/`. Every wrangler invocation in the repo
  // therefore passes `--config wrangler.toml` explicitly. See DEPLOY.md §2.0.
  //
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Why the `build` block below exists
  //
  // This check failed on every commit for weeks. The build log:
  //
  //   Executing user deploy command: npx wrangler versions upload
  //   ✘ [ERROR] The directory specified by the "assets.directory" field in
  //     your configuration file does not exist:
  //       /opt/buildhome/repo/_site_build
  //
  // `_site_build` is a build artifact, not a committed directory (see
  // .gitignore). The Cloudflare trigger has an EMPTY build command and goes
  // straight to the deploy command, so wrangler looked for a directory that
  // nothing had produced.
  //
  // The build command in the dashboard is one fix, but it is invisible from
  // the repo and silently un-versioned. `build.command` here is wrangler's
  // own custom-build hook and does the same job in a file that travels with
  // the code: wrangler runs it BEFORE reading `assets.directory`, so the
  // directory exists by the time it is needed.
  //
  // It runs even though this Worker has no `main`. An assets-only config
  // resolves its entry point to wrangler's bundled templates/no-op-worker.js
  // (see resolveEntryWithAssets), and runCustomBuild is called immediately
  // after that resolution — the entry-point assertion then passes trivially
  // because the no-op template always exists.
  //
  // The command mirrors .github/workflows/jekyll.yml exactly — same config
  // pair, same JEKYLL_ENV — so a preview renders what GitHub Pages serves
  // rather than a subtly different build. `cwd` is relative to the repo root,
  // which is the trigger's root directory.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "algosize-site",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-05-11",
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  "build": {
    "cwd": "site",
    "command": "bundle install && JEKYLL_ENV=production bundle exec jekyll build --config _config.yml,_config.production.yml --destination ../_site_build"
  },
  "assets": {
    "directory": "_site_build"
  },
  "compatibility_flags": [
    "nodejs_compat"
  ]
}
